{"step_index":1326,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T12:15:42Z","content":"That's a **CWE classification problem** — the model detects that code is vulnerable (correct ✅), but labels almost everything as **CWE-22** (Path Traversal) instead of the right CWE.\n\n### What's happening\n\n```\nModel sees:  pickle.loads(user_data)\nShould say:  CWE-502 (Deserialization)\nActually says: CWE-22 (Path Traversal) ← wrong label, right instinct\n```\n\nIt's like a doctor who correctly spots that a patient is sick, but keeps diagnosing everything as \"flu\" regardless of whether it's flu, a fracture, or a migraine.\n\n### Why\n\nYour training data had **45 vulnerability samples** — most were SQLi/Command Injection/Path Traversal heavy. The model learned:\n- \"dangerous code\" → **flag it** ✅ (correct behavior)\n- \"which CWE?\" → **CWE-22** because that's what it saw most ❌\n\nIt doesn't have enough examples of CWE-502, CWE-79, CWE-918, CWE-798 to classify them distinctly.\n\n### The fix\n\nAdd ~5-10 training samples **per missing CWE** to the dataset:\n\n| CWE | Needs | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| CWE-502 | 5-10 samples | `pickle.loads()`, `yaml.unsafe_load()`, Java `ObjectInputStream` |\n| CWE-79 | 5-10 samples | `innerHTML =`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, PHP `echo $_GET` |\n| CWE-918 | 5-10 samples | `requests.get(user_url)`, `http.Get(url)` |\n| CWE-798 | 5-10 samples | hardcoded API keys, passwords in source |\n| CWE-120 | 5-10 samples | `strcpy()`, `gets()`, buffer overflows |\n\nThat's ~30-50 new samples → one more training round on Colab → problem solved.\n\nWant me to prepare the expanded dataset and retrain?","thinking":"The user is asking about the misclassifications in the benchmark results. These are real issues with the model — it's over-classifying things as CWE-22 (Path Traversal) when they're actually different vulnerability types.\n\nThis is a model quality issue. The model detects that something is wrong (it flags everything as CVSS 9.8 Critical), but it keeps labeling things as CWE-22 instead of the correct CWE. This is because:\n\n1. The training data had too many CWE-22 examples, or\n2. The model learned \"CWE-22\" as a default fallback label\n3. With only 108 training samples and a 3B model, the CWE classification diversity is limited\nThis is fixable with more diverse training data covering these specific CWE categories, though it would need another training round. I should explain this clearly and simply."}
